@format 1
@id section-naming.common.keyboard-shortcut
@title Rename keyboard shortcut
@section Section naming
@area Common
@entry window-key,viewer-key
@summary Resolve the configurable SectionRename binding from either window or viewer keyboard input and dispatch nameSection to the active Split Flow.
@related section-naming.shortcut-popup
@tag section
@tag split
@tag rename
@tag shortcut

@alias window = Cadroue.UIShell.PMainWindow.PWindow
@alias viewer = Cadroue.UIShell.PPanels.PViewer
@alias binding = Cadroue.Infrastructure.LBinding
@alias flow = Cadroue.UIShell.PFlow.PFlow

[window-key] Main-window PreviewKeyDown <input>
~ This starts when the user presses a relevant shortcut key in the main window.
[Technical explanation]
- Forward main-window keyboard input into the shared shortcut processor @ window.PShortcutKeyHandle(...)
> key-entry

[viewer-key] Split viewer PreviewKeyDown <input>
~ This starts when the user presses a relevant shortcut key in the Split preview.
[Technical explanation]
- Forward viewer keyboard input through PViewerKeyDispatch and the window viewer-key adapter @ viewer.PViewerKeyHandle(...)
@ window.PShortcutViewerHandle(...)
> key-entry

[key-entry] Keyboard input convergence <junction>
> text-input-check

[text-input-check] Text-entry control active <decision>
~ The program checks text-entry control active.
[Technical explanation]
- PShortcutKeyProcess uses PWindowInputFind to ignore shortcut dispatch when the original source is a TextBoxBase or PasswordBox text-entry control @ window.PShortcutKeyProcess(...)
@ window.PWindowInputFind(...)
? "Text-entry control" > text-input
? "Shortcut-eligible input" > binding-lookup

[text-input] Leave keystroke to editor <output>
~ This path ends with leave keystroke to editor.
[Technical explanation]
= SectionRename is not dispatched while the keyboard event originates from a text-entry control.

[binding-lookup] Resolve configurable gesture <process>
~ This step handles resolve configurable gesture.
[Technical explanation]
- Format the key/modifier gesture and resolve it through LBindingCurrent; SectionRename defaults to A (default gesture A) but can be rebound @ window.PShortcutDispatch(...)
@ binding.LBindingGestureFormat(...)
@ binding.LBindingTokenFind(...)
> token-check

[token-check] SectionRename token resolved <decision>
~ The program checks which section-naming shortcut was pressed.
[Technical explanation]
- PShortcutRun routes SectionRename through PShortcutCodeRead as nameSection; unbound or other tokens leave this naming flow @ window.PShortcutRun(...)
@ window.PShortcutCodeRead(...)
? "SectionRename" > flow-dispatch
? "Other or unbound" > other

[other] Outside section naming <output>
~ This path ends with outside section naming.
[Technical explanation]
= No section-name popup is requested by this gesture.

[flow-dispatch] Dispatch nameSection to active Flow <decision>
~ The program checks dispatch nameSection to active Flow.
[Technical explanation]
- PFlowShortcutDispatch first requires command-active Flow state and a non-null spool, then accepts nameSection only while section UI is active and calls PFlowNameShow; it does not itself require pFlowSectionEditable @ flow.PFlowShortcutDispatch(...) / nameSection
@ flow.PFlowNameShow(...)
? "Dispatch conditions fail" > blocked
? "nameSection accepted" > map:section-naming.shortcut-popup

[blocked] Rename shortcut not handled <output>
~ This path ends with rename shortcut not handled.
[Technical explanation]
= The popup does not open.
